// Practice quiz: Interfaces and default methods
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/interfaces-and-default-methods

::o1 many implementations::Payment is an interface with pay(). Card and Wallet implement it. What can a method with a Payment parameter accept? {
~Only objects created with new Payment()#Not quite. An interface cannot be instantiated.
~Only Card, the first implementing class#Not quite. Every implementing class qualifies.
=Any Card or Wallet object, or any other class implementing Payment#Correct. Code that uses the interface type works with every implementation.
~Nothing until Payment extends a class#Not quite. Interfaces do not need a superclass.
}

::o1 several interfaces::How many interfaces can one class implement? {
~Only one#Not quite. That limit applies to extends, not implements.
~Two at most#Not quite. There is no such limit.
=As many as it needs#Correct. A class has one superclass but may implement many interfaces.
~None if it already extends a class#Not quite. A class can extend a class and implement interfaces together.
}

::o2 own method wins::An interface gives greet() a default body, and class A implements the interface and declares its own greet(). Which runs for a call on an A object? {
~The default greet()#Not quite. The default is used only when the class does not provide its own.
~Both, default first#Not quite. Only one runs unless A calls the default explicitly.
~It does not compile#Not quite. Overriding a default method is legal.
=A's own greet()#Correct. A class's method overrides an inherited default method.
}

::o2 conflicting defaults::Class C implements interfaces I and J, and both supply a default hello() with the same signature. What must C do? {
=Override hello() itself, optionally calling I.super.hello() or J.super.hello()#Correct. Java will not pick between two unrelated defaults for you.
~Nothing; the first interface listed wins#Not quite. Order in the implements list does not decide.
~Nothing; the last interface listed wins#Not quite. Order does not decide in either direction.
~Remove one interface#Not quite. Overriding resolves the conflict without removing either.
}

::o3 interface choice::Unrelated classes such as Invoice and Photo must both be printable, and they share no fields. Which type fits? {
~An abstract class Printable#Not quite. Each class could then extend nothing else, and there is no shared state to justify it.
~A concrete class Printable#Not quite. That would force unrelated classes into one hierarchy.
~A record#Not quite. Records model data and cannot be extended.
=An interface Printable#Correct. An interface lets unrelated classes share a capability without a common superclass.
}

::o3 fields in interfaces::A design needs each object to keep its own count field in the shared supertype. Which supertype can hold that? {
~An interface with an int count field#Not quite. That field would be a shared constant, not per object.
=An abstract class#Correct. Interface fields are implicitly public, static and final, so they cannot hold per-object state.
~An interface with a default method that stores count#Not quite. Default methods have no instance field to store it in.
~Either one equally#Not quite. Only a class can declare instance fields.
}
